ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE WATER LEISURE SAFETY ACT

Chapter 1 General Provisions

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to provide for matters delegated by the Water Leisure Safety Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Types of Water Leisure Craft)
(1) “Craft prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as personal watercraft, motorboats, rubber boats, and sailing yachts (meaning yachts equipped with sails and an engine)” in subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Water Leisure Safety Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Act”) means the following water leisure craft:
1. Personal watercraft;
2. Motorboats;
3. Rubber boats;
4. Sailing yachts (meaning yachts equipped with sails and an engine; hereinafter the same shall apply);
5. Water scooters;
6. Air-cushion craft (hovercraft);
7. Amphibious craft;
8. Other water leisure craft having a structure, form, propulsion engine, or operating method similar to craft under subparagraphs 1 through 7, as determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.
(2) “Craft prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act means the following water leisure craft:
1. Water skis (including cable water skis; hereinafter the same shall apply);
2. Parasails;
3. Rowing craft;
4. Kayaks;
5. Canoes;
6. Water sleds;
7. Water bicycles;
8. Surfboards;
9. Rowboats;
10. Non-powered yachts;
11. Windsurfing equipment;
12. Wakeboards (including cable wakeboards);
13. Kiteboards;
14. Stationary inflatable tubes;
15. Flyboards;
16. Paddleboards;
17. Other water leisure craft having a structure, form, or operating method similar to craft under subparagraphs 1 through 16, as determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.

Article 3 (Establishment and Implementation of Master Plans for Water Leisure Safety Management)
(1) Where necessary for establishing and implementing a master plan for water leisure safety management under Article 4 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “master plan”), the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may request the head of a relevant central administrative agency or local government to submit relevant materials. In such cases, the head of the relevant central administrative agency or local government receiving the request shall comply unless there are special circumstances.
(2) Where considered necessary for establishing and implementing a master plan, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may hear the opinions of relevant experts and interested parties.
(3) Upon establishing a master plan, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall publicly notify its contents in the Official Gazette or post them on the comprehensive water leisure information system under Article 36 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “comprehensive water leisure information system”) or the Korea Coast Guard's website.

Chapter 2 Operator's Licenses

Article 4 (Craft Requiring Operator's Licenses and License Standards)
(1) Power-driven water leisure craft for which an operator must obtain a power-driven water leisure craft operator's license under Article 5 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “operator's license”) shall be craft falling under the subparagraphs of Article 2 (1) whose propulsion engine has a maximum output of at least 5 horsepower (meaning at least 3.75 kilowatts where output is expressed in kilowatts).
(2) The persons to whom operator's licenses are issued shall be classified as follows:
1. General operator's licenses:
(a) Class 1 operator's license: Personnel of water leisure businesses registered under Article 37 (1) of the Act, and examiners of examination agencies under Article 17 (1) 2 and paragraph 1 (b) of Annex 9;
(b) Class 2 operator's license: Persons intending to operate power-driven water leisure craft requiring an operator's license under paragraph (1) (excluding sailing yachts);
2. Yacht operator's license: Persons intending to operate sailing yachts.

Article 5 (Disqualifications for Operator's Licenses)
(1) “Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7 (1) 2 of the Act means persons with a mental illness consisting of dementia, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, bipolar affective disorder (manic depression), recurrent depressive disorder, or alcoholism, who are considered by a specialist in the relevant field to be unable to operate power-driven water leisure craft normally.
(2) “Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7 (1) 3 of the Act means persons addicted to narcotics, psychotropic drugs, or cannabis under subparagraphs 2 through 4 of Article 2 of the Narcotics Control Act, who are considered by a specialist in the relevant field to be unable to operate power-driven water leisure craft normally.

Article 6 (Notification of Personal Information concerning Disqualifications for Operator's Licenses)
(1) “Heads of institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7 (2) of the Act means the following persons:
1. Minister of Health and Welfare;
2. Commissioner of the Military Manpower Administration;
3. Special Metropolitan City Mayors, Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayors, Do Governors, and Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as “Mayors/Do Governors”), or heads of Sis/Guns and autonomous Gus;
4. Chief of Staff of the Army, Navy, or Air Force, or Commandant of the Marine Corps;
5. Heads of mental healthcare institutions under subparagraph 5 of Article 3 of the Act on the Improvement of Mental Health and the Support for Welfare Services for Mental Patients (hereinafter referred to as “heads of mental healthcare institutions”).
(2) Personal information that persons under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) must notify to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard under Article 7 (2) of the Act shall be information concerning disqualifications for operator's licenses under paragraph (1) 2 and 3 of that Article, with the contents specified in Annex 1.
(3) Persons under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) shall notify the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard of personal information under paragraph (2) at least once every 6 months, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 7 (Administration of License Examinations)
(1) When intending to administer a license examination under Article 8 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “license examination”), the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall publicly announce the date, time, place, examination subjects, and other matters necessary for administration of the examination, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) A person intending to take a license examination shall submit an application to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 8 (Subjects and Methods of Written Examinations)
(1) The subjects of the written portion of a license examination (hereinafter referred to as a “written examination”) shall be as specified in Annex 2.
(2) Written examinations shall be objective tests; provided, a person considered to have difficulty taking a written examination because of illiteracy may substitute an oral examination for the written examination.
(3) The passing standards for written examinations shall be classified as follows:
1. General operator's licenses: At least 70 out of 100 points for a class 1 operator's license, and at least 60 out of 100 points for a class 2 operator's license;
2. Yacht operator's license: At least 70 out of 100 points.
(4) A person who has passed a written examination shall be exempt from the written examination only in license examinations administered within 1 year from the date of passing.
(5) A person who fails a written examination may retake it from the day following the date of failure.

Article 9 (Methods of Practical Examinations)
(1) The practical portion of a license examination (hereinafter referred to as a “practical examination”) shall be administered only to persons who have passed or are exempted from the written examination.
(2) The passing standards for practical examinations shall be classified as follows:
1. General operator's licenses: At least 80 out of 100 points for a class 1 operator's license, and at least 60 out of 100 points for a class 2 operator's license;
2. Yacht operator's license: At least 60 out of 100 points.
(3) Scoring standards and operating courses for practical examinations shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(4) When administering a practical examination, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall require candidates to use power-driven water leisure craft for practical examinations meeting the specifications in Annex 3; provided, if a craft separately prepared by a candidate meets those specifications, the Commissioner may allow that craft to be used in the practical examination.
(5) A person who fails a practical examination may retake it from the second day after the date of failure.
(6) Matters necessary for administration of practical examinations, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 10 (Exemptions from License Examinations)
(1) “Sports-related organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 9 (1) 1 of the Act means sports organizations under subparagraph 11 of Article 2 of the National Sports Promotion Act.
(2) “Departments related to power-driven water leisure craft prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 9 (1) 2 (a) of the Act means departments requiring completion of at least 6 credits in subjects related to power-driven water leisure craft.
(3) “Licenses prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 9 (1) 3 of the Act means licenses for navigation officers, engineer officers, operating officers, wing-in-ground craft pilots, or small vessel operators under Article 4 (2) 1, 2, 4, 5, or 6 of the Ship Personnel Act.
(4) Standards for exemptions from license examination subjects under Article 9 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 4.

Article 11 (Designation of License Examination Exemption Education Institutions)
(1) Under Article 9 (1) 5 of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may designate an institution or organization meeting all of the following standards as an institution or organization providing education that exempts persons from all license examination subjects (limited to class 2 operator's licenses and yacht operator's licenses) (hereinafter referred to as a “license examination exemption education institution”):
1. It shall be an institution or organization falling under any of the following items:
(a) An institution operating and managing watercraft similar to power-driven water leisure craft for the performance of police, coast guard, firefighting, or national defense duties;
(b) A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions or a local government;
(c) A school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act operating a curriculum related to power-driven water leisure craft;
(d) Another education institution or organization related to water leisure activities that is recognized by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard;
2. It shall meet the personnel, equipment, and facility standards in Annex 5.
(2) A person intending to obtain designation as a license examination exemption education institution shall apply to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) When designating a license examination exemption education institution, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall issue the designation certificate prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to the applicant, and shall publicly notify the designation in the Official Gazette or post it on the comprehensive water leisure information system or the Korea Coast Guard's website.
(4) Educational curricula that license examination exemption education institutions must operate under Article 9 (3) of the Act shall be classified as follows:
1. For exemption from the class 2 operator's license examination: Water leisure statutes and regulations, basic knowledge of waters, lifesaving and first aid, an introduction to motorboats, navigation and engines, and craft handling;
2. For exemption from the yacht operator's license examination: Water leisure statutes and regulations, basic knowledge of waters, lifesaving and first aid, an introduction to yachts, navigation and engines, and sailing techniques.
(5) Matters necessary for designation and educational operations of license examination exemption education institutions, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 12 (Standards for Revocation of Designation of License Examination Exemption Education Institutions)
(1) Detailed standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of license examination exemption education institutions under Article 10 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 6.
(2) A person whose designation as a license examination exemption education institution is revoked under Article 10 (1) of the Act shall return the designation certificate under Article 11 (3) to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.

Article 13 (Deferral of Renewal of Operator's License Certificates)
(1) “Military service or another reason prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the proviso to the part of Article 12 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means any of the following cases:
1. Military service (including service converted to service as an obligatory firefighter or auxiliary police officer under Article 25 of the Military Service Act), or service as an alternative service member under the Act on Assignment to and Performance of Alternative Service;
2. Staying abroad;
3. Having suffered a calamity or disaster;
4. Being unable to move due to illness or injury;
5. Deprivation of physical liberty under statutes and regulations;
6. Other circumstances reasonably recognized as unavoidable according to generally accepted social norms.
(2) A person intending to renew a power-driven water leisure craft operator's license certificate (hereinafter referred to as a “license certificate”) in advance or defer renewal under the proviso to the part of Article 12 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs shall submit an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, with documents proving the grounds attached, to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard by the dates classified as follows:
1. Advance renewal: By the day preceding the beginning of the renewal period under the subparagraphs of Article 12 (1) of the Act;
2. Deferral of renewal: By the last day of the renewal period under the subparagraphs of Article 12 (1) of the Act.
(3) If the application under paragraph (2) is found justified, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may renew and issue the license certificate in advance or defer its renewal.
(4) A person whose license certificate renewal is deferred under paragraph (3) shall renew it within 3 months from the date the grounds for deferral cease to exist.

Article 14 (Persons Exempt from Water Safety Education)
“Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the proviso to the part of Article 13 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. A person who received either of the following types of education within the 6 months counted backward from the date of submission of the license examination application under Article 8 of the Act or the beginning of the renewal period under the subparagraphs of Article 12 (1) of the Act:
(a) Water safety education under Article 13 (1) of the Act;
(b) Basic safety education or advanced safety education under Article 43 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Seafarers Act;
2. A person exempted from all subjects of the class 2 operator's license examination or yacht operator's license examination under Article 9 (1) 5 of the Act;
3. A person who received personnel training under Article 19 of the Act within the 6 months counted backward from the last day of the renewal period under the subparagraphs of Article 12 (1) of the Act.

Article 15 (Designation of Entrusted Safety Education Institutions)
(1) Under Article 13 (2) of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may designate an institution or organization meeting all of the following standards as an institution or organization entrusted to provide water safety education under Article 13 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “entrusted safety education institution”):
1. It shall fall under any item of Article 11 (1) 1;
2. Its designation as an entrusted safety education institution shall not have been revoked within the most recent 3 years;
3. It shall meet the personnel, facility, and equipment standards in Annex 7.
(2) A person intending to obtain designation as an entrusted safety education institution shall apply to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) When designating an entrusted safety education institution, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall issue the designation certificate prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to the applicant, and shall publicly notify the designation in the Official Gazette or post it on the comprehensive water leisure information system or the Korea Coast Guard's website.
(4) Matters necessary for designation of entrusted safety education institutions and education provided by those institutions, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 16 (Standards for Revocation of Designation of Entrusted Safety Education Institutions)
(1) Detailed standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of entrusted safety education institutions under Article 14 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 8.
(2) A person whose designation as an entrusted safety education institution is revoked under Article 14 (1) of the Act shall return the designation certificate under Article 15 (3) to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.

Article 17 (Designation of Examination Agencies)
(1) Under Article 18 (1) of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may designate an institution or organization meeting all of the following standards as an institution or organization performing license examination services on the Commissioner's behalf (hereinafter referred to as an “examination agency”):
1. It shall fall under any item of Article 11 (1) 1;
2. It shall meet the personnel, equipment, and facility standards in Annex 9 for each examination site.
(2) A person intending to obtain designation as an examination agency shall apply to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) When designating an examination agency, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall issue the designation certificate prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to the applicant, and shall publicly notify the designation in the Official Gazette or post it on the comprehensive water leisure information system or the Korea Coast Guard's website.

Article 18 (Standards for Revocation of Designation of Examination Agencies)
(1) Detailed standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of examination agencies under Article 18 (2) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 10.
(2) A person whose designation as an examination agency is revoked under Article 18 (2) of the Act shall return the designation certificate under Article 17 (3) to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.

Article 19 (Supervision of Examination Agencies)
(1) An examination agency shall submit its performance of license examination services under Article 18 (3) of the Act to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard within 14 days from the end of each half-year. In such cases, the performance report may be submitted through the comprehensive water leisure information system.
(2) Under Article 18 (4) of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may request an examination agency to submit materials necessary for verification or supervision of its performance of services.

Article 20 (Compliance with Navigation Methods and Other Requirements)
(1) A person engaging in water leisure activities shall comply with the requirements concerning navigation methods, craft speeds, and other matters specified in Annex 11 pursuant to Article 21 of the Act.
(2) When using power-driven water leisure craft required to be registered under Article 6 of the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft (hereinafter referred to as “power-driven water leisure craft subject to registration”), a person under paragraph (1) shall navigate only within the navigation area designated under Article 16 (2) of that Act. This shall not apply in either of the following cases:
1. Navigating coastal waters within 10 nautical miles [5 nautical miles for power-driven water leisure craft using engines prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries] from the outer boundary of sheltered waters and the nearest land or island, using power-driven water leisure craft whose navigation area has been designated as sheltered waters under Article 16 (2) of the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft and subparagraph 1 (a) of Article 2 of its Enforcement Decree;
2. Having submitted to the chief of the competent coast guard station a navigation report prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, in order to navigate simultaneously within 500 meters of power-driven water leisure craft whose navigation area has been designated as coastal waters or beyond, using power-driven water leisure craft whose navigation area has been designated as sheltered waters under Article 16 (2) of the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft and subparagraph 1 (a) of Article 2 of its Enforcement Decree.

Article 21 (Exceptions to Restrictions on Water Leisure Activities)
“Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the proviso to the part of Article 22 of the Act other than its subparagraphs means cases where an advisory for high waves, storm surges, heavy rain, heavy snow, or strong winds among special weather alerts is in effect and a person has submitted a report on activities under a special weather alert, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, to the chief of the competent coast guard station or the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Governor of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province, or the head of the competent Si, Gun, or Gu (the head of a Gu means the head of an autonomous Gu, and, for the Han River within the jurisdiction of Seoul Special Metropolitan City, means the head of the agency responsible for managing the Han River of Seoul Special Metropolitan City; hereinafter referred to as the “head of a Si, Gun, or Gu”), in order to engage in water leisure activities.

Article 22 (Standards for Calculating Passenger Capacity of Water Leisure Craft)
(1) The passenger capacity of water leisure craft under Article 29 of the Act shall be the capacity determined through the safety inspection under Article 15 of the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft.
(2) The passenger capacity of water leisure craft other than power-driven water leisure craft subject to registration shall be calculated in accordance with standards determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, taking account of the number of seats, shape, and other characteristics of the craft concerned.
(3) When calculating passenger capacity under paragraphs (1) and (2), persons who have boarded for rescue on the water or other unavoidable reasons shall not count toward that capacity.

CHAPTER IV SAFETY MANAGEMENT

Article 23 (Korea Water Leisure Safety Association)
(1) The articles of association of the Korea Water Leisure Safety Association under Article 34 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Association”) shall include the following matters:
1. Purpose;
2. Name;
3. Location of offices (including branches and business offices);
4. Business and its execution;
5. Membership qualifications, admission, withdrawal, rights, and duties;
6. Officers and employees;
7. General meetings and the board of directors;
8. Bodies and organization;
9. Assets and accounting;
10. Amendment of the articles of association;
11. Methods of public announcement.
(2) The Association shall perform the following services under Article 34 (1) of the Act:
1. Research, development, and publicity concerning systems for managing safety in water leisure activities;
2. Development and dissemination of safety education programs for water leisure activities;
3. Education, training, and guidance for persons working in fields related to water leisure safety;
4. Investigation and research into safety technology for water leisure craft;
5. Domestic and international cooperation concerning the water leisure industry;
6. Surveys of the current state of the water leisure industry and statistical surveys.
(3) Any of the following persons may become a member of the Association:
1. A water leisure business operator;
2. A representative of an institution, corporation, or organization related to water leisure activities;
3. Any other person designated as a member by the Association's articles of association.
(4) The Association shall report the following matters to the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard:
1. Business operations;
2. Financial operations;
3. Organizational operations;
4. Other matters the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard considers necessary for supervising the Association.

Article 24 (Composition and Other Matters of Water Leisure Activity Safety Councils)
(1) A water leisure activity safety council under Article 35 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “safety council”) shall discuss the following matters:
1. Improvement and supplementation of systems for managing safety in water leisure activities;
2. Cooperation on major policies and services related to safety in water leisure activities;
3. Prevention of and response to accidents in water leisure activities;
4. Other matters related to safety in water leisure activities that require discussion.
(2) Matters concerning the composition and operation of a safety council shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government in accordance with the following standards:
1. The safety council shall consist of not more than 17 members, including one chairperson;
2. The chairperson shall be specified by ordinance from among the deputy mayors or deputy governors of the relevant local government, or shall be appointed by the relevant Mayor/Do Governor;
3. The members shall consist of public officials of that local government responsible for water leisure safety services, public officials of relevant administrative agencies performing services related to water leisure safety, and civilian members with knowledge and experience in water leisure safety;
4. Matters necessary for holding meetings, including the person authorized to convene meetings and the convening procedure, shall be prescribed.

CHAPTER V WATER LEISURE BUSINESSES

Article 25 (Subjects and Procedures of Safety Checks)
(1) The subjects and items of safety checks under Article 43 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “safety checks”) shall be as follows:
1. Safety of water leisure craft (excluding power-driven water leisure craft subject to safety inspection under Article 15 of the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft);
2. Whether facilities, equipment, and other items installed at the premises of a water leisure business under Article 37 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “water leisure business”) meet the registration standards;
3. Duties of water leisure business operators and their personnel to take the measures under the subparagraphs of Article 44 (1) of the Act;
4. Duties to comply with qualification and placement standards for lifeguards and rafting guides under Article 44 (1) 5 of the Act;
5. Duties of water leisure business operators and their personnel to comply with restrictions on conduct and other requirements under the subparagraphs of Article 44 (2) of the Act.
(2) When conducting a safety check under Article 43 (1) of the Act, the chief of a coast guard station or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu shall notify the person subject to the safety check in advance of its purpose, subjects, date and time, and other relevant matters. Advance notice may be omitted in an urgent case, such as where a calamity or disaster is anticipated, or where it is deemed that advance notice would prevent the purpose of the safety check from being achieved because of destruction of evidence or similar acts.
(3) When disclosing the results of a safety check under Article 43 (1) of the Act, the chief of a coast guard station or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu shall post the results, including the following matters, on the relevant agency's website for at least 2 months:
1. Name and location of the water leisure craft or water leisure facility checked;
2. Period during which the safety check was conducted;
3. Details of the safety check results and planned measures;
4. Other matters concerning the safety check results that the chief of the coast guard station or the head of the Si, Gun, or Gu considers necessary to disclose.

Article 26 (Qualification Standards for Lifeguards and Rafting Guides)
(1) A lifeguard or rafting guide under Article 44 (1) 5 of the Act shall be a person holding the qualifications classified as follows: <Amended on December 16, 2025>
1. Lifeguard: A person qualified as a water rescue specialist under Article 30-2 of the Act on Search and Rescue on the Water;
2. Rafting guide: A person who has acquired a rafting guide qualification after completing a lifesaving education course operated by an institution, organization, or corporation designated by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard from among institutions, organizations, or corporations meeting the designation standards for rafting guide education institutions in Annex 12 (hereinafter referred to in this Article as an “education institution”).
(2) Matters necessary for designation and revocation of designation of education institutions, their management and supervision, operation of lifesaving education courses, and other related matters shall be determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard.
(3) Lifeguards shall be stationed within the business area of the relevant water leisure business.
(4) At least one rafting guide shall board each rafting craft in operation to check safety conditions in the business area and the safety of passengers. If all of the following requirements are met, a rafting guide may instead board another rafting craft and navigate nearby to check safety conditions in the business area and the safety of passengers:
1. The head of the Si, Gun, or Gu having jurisdiction over the navigated waters recognizes that this will not impede the prevention of danger, taking into account the water depth, current speed, navigation distance, strength of rapids, and other conditions of the premises and business area concerned;
2. The passenger capacity of each rafting craft is not more than 4 persons.
(5) Under the proviso to paragraph (4), the head of the Si, Gun, or Gu having jurisdiction over the navigated waters shall determine, within a range of 2 to 5, the number of rafting craft that may be operated while one rafting guide navigates nearby.
(6) An emergency rescue boat stationed under Article 44 (1) 6 of the Act (meaning power-driven water leisure craft used to patrol water leisure business premises and their business areas and to save lives; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall be power-driven water leisure craft suitable for lifesaving and shall bear markings identifying it as an emergency rescue boat.

Article 27 (Scope of Dangerous Goods)
“Explosives, inflammable substances, and other dangerous goods prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 44 (2) 6 of the Act means dangerous goods under Article 41 (3) of the Ship Safety Act.

Article 28 (Water Leisure Craft Subject to Restrictions on Business Operations)
“Water leisure craft prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as water leisure craft through which users' bodies come into direct contact with the surface of the water” in the proviso to the part of Article 46 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means scooters, water skis, parasails, surfboards, and other water leisure craft through which the bodies of persons engaging in water leisure activities come into contact with the surface of the water during those activities.

Article 29 (Submission of Materials)
Under Article 47 of the Act, the chief of a coast guard station or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu may require a water leisure business operator to submit the following documents:
1. Documents proving that the measures under the subparagraphs of Article 44 (1) of the Act have been taken;
2. Documents concerning enrollment in insurance or mutual aid under Article 49 (2) of the Act.

CHAPTER VI INSURANCE

Article 30 (Enrollment in Insurance or Mutual Aid by Owners of Power-Driven Water Leisure Craft)
Under Article 49 (1) of the Act, the owner of power-driven water leisure craft subject to registration shall enroll in insurance or mutual aid (hereinafter referred to as “insurance or mutual aid”) meeting all of the following requirements:
1. Coverage period: Coverage shall be maintained continuously throughout the registration period of the power-driven water leisure craft;
2. Coverage amount: The amount shall be at least the amount under Article 3 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Guarantee of Automobile Accident Compensation Act.

Article 31 (Enrollment in Insurance or Mutual Aid by Water Leisure Business Operators)
Under Article 49 (2) of the Act, a water leisure business operator shall enroll in insurance or mutual aid meeting all of the following requirements:
1. Coverage period: Coverage shall be maintained continuously throughout the business period of the water leisure business operator;
2. Insured persons or persons covered by mutual aid: Persons working in the water leisure business or users of water leisure craft shall be the insured persons or persons covered by mutual aid;
3. Coverage amount: The amount shall be at least the amount under Article 3 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Guarantee of Automobile Accident Compensation Act.

Article 32 (Provision of Information on Enrollment in Insurance or Mutual Aid)
Under Article 50 of the Act, a water leisure business operator shall display the following matters at a conspicuous place within the business premises:
1. Coverage period of insurance or mutual aid;
2. Insured persons or persons covered by the insurance or mutual aid enrolled in;
3. Coverage amount of the insurance or mutual aid.

Article 33 (Establishment and Operation of the Insurance or Mutual Aid Enrollment Management Computer Network)
(1) Under Article 52 (1) of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall perform the following services to establish and operate the insurance or mutual aid enrollment management computer network (hereinafter referred to as the “enrollment management computer network”):
1. Configuration, management, and improvement of the enrollment management computer network;
2. Establishment, dissemination, and operation of a database of insurance or mutual aid enrollment information;
3. Installation and management of computers, communication equipment, and other facilities for operating the enrollment management computer network;
4. Other services necessary for establishing and operating the enrollment management computer network.
(2) The Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard may formulate and implement guidelines for operating the enrollment management computer network to perform the services under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) smoothly.
(3) “Information prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the former part of Article 52 (2) of the Act means information concerning enrollment in insurance or mutual aid under Article 49 of the Act and changes to such information. <Newly inserted on June 2, 2025>

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 34 (Standards for Imposing Penalty Surcharges)
The standards for imposing penalty surcharges under Article 56 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 13.

Article 35 (Imposition and Payment of Penalty Surcharges)
(1) When imposing a penalty surcharge under Article 56 (1) of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall notify the person subject to the surcharge in writing, specifying the type of violation and the amount of the penalty surcharge concerned.
(2) A person notified under paragraph (1) shall pay the penalty surcharge to the collecting institution designated by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard within 20 days from the date of receipt of the notice.
(3) A collecting institution that receives a penalty surcharge under paragraph (2) shall issue a receipt to the payer and notify the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard of payment without delay.

Article 36 (Delegation of Authority)
Under Article 59 of the Act, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall delegate authority to commissioners of regional coast guards or chiefs of coast guard stations as follows:
1. Commissioners of regional coast guards: Guidance and supervision necessary for formulating and implementing water leisure safety management implementation plans under Article 4 (5) of the Act;
2. Chiefs of coast guard stations: The following authority:
(a) Renewal of license certificates under Article 12 of the Act;
(b) Issuance and reissuance of license certificates under Article 15 of the Act;
(c) Revocation and suspension of operator's licenses under Article 17 (1) of the Act.

Article 37 (Processing of Sensitive Information and Personally Identifiable Information)
(1) Where unavoidable for performing any of the following duties, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard (including persons performing services on the Commissioner's behalf under Article 18 (1) of the Act), the chief of a coast guard station, or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu may process information on health under Article 23 of the Personal Information Protection Act (limited to the duties in subparagraphs 1, 2, 5, and 6), information constituting criminal history records under subparagraph 2 of Article 18 of the Enforcement Decree of that Act (limited to the duties in subparagraphs 5 through 11), and materials containing resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of that Decree:
1. Verification of grounds for disqualification from an operator's license under Article 7 (1) of the Act;
2. Receipt of applications for license examinations under Article 8 (3) of the Act;
3. Renewal of license certificates under Article 12 of the Act;
4. Issuance and reissuance of license certificates under Article 15 of the Act;
5. Revocation and suspension of operator's licenses under Article 17 (1) of the Act;
6. Establishment and operation of the comprehensive water leisure information system under Article 36 of the Act;
7. Registration and registration of changes of water leisure businesses under Article 37 (1) and (2) of the Act;
8. Renewal registration of water leisure businesses under Article 38 (2) of the Act;
9. Verification of grounds for disqualification from water leisure business registration under Article 39 of the Act;
10. Acceptance of reports on resumption of water leisure businesses under Article 41 (2) of the Act;
11. Revocation of water leisure business registration under Article 48 of the Act.
(2) Where unavoidable for performing any of the following duties, the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard (including persons entrusted with the Commissioner's services under Article 13 (2) of the Act), the commissioner of a regional coast guard, the chief of a coast guard station, or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu may process materials containing resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act: <Amended on June 2, 2025>
1. Provision of water safety education under Article 13 (1) of the Act;
2. Provision of personnel training under Article 19 (1) of the Act;
3. Requests for provision of materials or information under Article 51 (1) of the Act;
4. Establishment and operation of the enrollment management computer network under Article 52 (1) of the Act;
5. Measures concerning persons not enrolled in insurance or mutual aid under Article 53 (2) of the Act.
(3) Where unavoidable for providing the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard, commissioners of regional coast guards, chiefs of coast guard stations, or heads of Si, Gun, or Gu with materials or information concerning enrollment in insurance or mutual aid under Article 51 (2) of the Act, insurance companies and mutual aid business operators under Article 51 (1) of the Act, or insurance associations and similar organizations under Section 1 of Chapter XI of the Insurance Business Act, may process materials containing resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act.

Article 38 (Review of Regulations)
The Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall review the appropriateness of each of the following matters every 3 years from January 1, 2022 (meaning by the day preceding the same day as the base date in every third year), and take measures such as improvements:
1. Standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of license examination exemption education institutions under Article 12 and Annex 6;
2. Standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of entrusted safety education institutions under Article 16 and Annex 8;
3. Standards for revocation of designation and suspension of services of examination agencies under Article 18 and Annex 10;
4. Requirements concerning navigation methods, craft speeds, and other matters under Article 20 and Annex 11.

