■ Enforcement Decree of the Water Leisure Safety Act [Annex 5]
Personnel, Equipment, and Facility Standards for License Examination Exemption Education Institutions (Related to Article 11 (1) 2)

Personnel standards: The following personnel shall be employed:
(a) One responsible manager with at least 5 years of experience in water leisure related services determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard;
(b) At least one instructor falling under any of the following:
1) A person who has acquired a class 1 operator's license and the lifeguard qualification under Article 26 (1) 1 (limited to cases of providing education for a class 2 operator's license);
2) A person who has acquired a yacht operator's license and the lifeguard qualification under Article 26 (1) 1 (limited to cases of providing education for a yacht operator's license);
3) A person who graduated from a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act with a major in water leisure, or a person recognized under statutes and regulations as having equivalent academic qualifications;
4) A person with at least 3 years of experience teaching water leisure at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act or a school under Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
5) A person with at least 3 years of experience as a responsible manager under subparagraph 1 (a) of Annex 9 at an examination agency;
(c) At least one instructor falling under any of the following:
1) A person holding a license as a class 3 or higher deck officer, class 3 or higher marine engineer, or class 3 or higher ship operator under Article 4 (2) of the Ship Officers Act;
2) A person with at least 3 years of experience in water safety management services in the armed forces, police, or a fire service agency;
3) A person with at least 3 years of experience in water safety management services at an institution or organization established under statutes and regulations;
4) A person with at least 5 years of experience as an examiner under subparagraph 1 (b) of Annex 9 at an examination agency;
5) A person with at least 5 years of experience as an instructor at a license examination exemption education institution.

2. Equipment standards: All of the following equipment shall be provided:
(a) At least one power-driven water leisure craft for practical examinations meeting the specifications under Article 9 (4) and Annex 3;
(b) At least one emergency rescue boat. Its speed shall be at least 20 knots and its passenger capacity shall be at least 4 persons;
(c) At least 20 life jackets;
(d) At least 5 lifebuoys;
(e) At least 3 fire extinguishers;
(f) At least 3 spare oars;
(g) Distress signaling equipment (self-igniting lights and red signal flares) and first aid equipment (emergency medicines and a stretcher);
(h) At least one upper-body manikin for lifesaving education.

3. Facility standards: All of the following facilities shall be provided:
(a) Theory classroom: An area of at least 60 square meters and capacity for at least 50 persons;
(b) Practical training site: Mooring facilities for power-driven water leisure craft and waiting facilities for trainees;
(c) Administrative office: An area of at least 20 square meters;
(d) Parking lot: Space to park at least 10 passenger cars;
(e) Other amenities: A lounge and toilets available to trainees. Separate toilets shall be provided for men and women.

Notes
1. If rights to use equipment and facilities are secured through a lease agreement or similar arrangement and this is proved by documents, the relevant equipment and facilities shall be deemed provided for the term of that agreement.
2. Equipment such as closed-circuit television (CCTV) capable of verifying whether the relevant trainees have completed the education shall be installed in the theory classroom and at the practical training site.

■ Enforcement Decree of the Water Leisure Safety Act [Annex 6]
Detailed Standards for Revocation of Designation and Suspension of Services of License Examination Exemption Education Institutions (Related to Article 12 (1))

1. General standards
(a) If there are two or more violations and their respective disposition standards differ, the heavier disposition standard shall apply. If two or more disposition standards all require suspension of services, the heavier disposition standard shall be increased by up to one-half, without exceeding the sum of the periods under the respective disposition standards.
(b) Standards for administrative dispositions based on the number of violations shall apply where an administrative disposition (including a disposition imposing a penalty surcharge; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Annex) has been received for the same violation within the most recent 1 year. The period shall be calculated by reference to the date the administrative disposition was received for the violation and the date on which the same violation committed again after that disposition was detected.
(c) When imposing an aggravated administrative disposition under item (b), the applicable violation level shall be the level immediately following that of the disposition preceding the violation concerned (the highest level if two or more administrative dispositions were imposed within the period under item (b)).
(d) The authority imposing the disposition may reduce the suspension period under the individual standards in paragraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases:
1) Where the violation is recognized as resulting from minor negligence or error;
2) Where the violator immediately corrects or remedies the violation and thereby ends the state of noncompliance with the Act;
3) Where a reduction of the suspension period is otherwise considered necessary in view of the nature, severity, motive, consequences, or other circumstances of the violation.
(e) The authority imposing the disposition may increase the suspension period under the individual standards in paragraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases. Even when increased, the suspension period shall not exceed 6 months:
1) Where the nature and severity of the violation are serious and the harm to users or others is recognized as substantial;
2) Where the state of noncompliance with the Act has lasted at least 6 months;
3) Where an increase in the suspension period is otherwise considered necessary in view of the severity, motive, consequences, or other circumstances of the violation.

2. Individual standards

