■ Enforcement Decree of the Water Leisure Safety Act [Annex 11] <Amended on June 2, 2025>
Requirements concerning Navigation Methods, Craft Speeds, and Other Matters (Related to Article 20 (1))

1. Navigation methods
(a) A proper lookout shall be maintained at all times by sight, hearing, and all other available means appropriate to the prevailing circumstances, so as to make a full assessment of surrounding conditions and the risk of collision with other water leisure craft or ships (hereinafter referred to in this Annex as “water leisure craft or ships”).
(b) Where there is a risk of a head-on collision with other water leisure craft or ships, the other party shall be notified by appropriate means, such as vocal signals or hand signals, and course shall be altered to starboard (the side on the right when facing the bow).
(c) Where there is a risk of collision when crossing the course of other water leisure craft or ships, the water leisure craft that has the other water leisure craft or ship on its right shall keep out of the way.
(d) When navigating in the same direction as other water leisure craft or ships, navigation within 2 meters of them is prohibited.
(e) When intending to overtake other water leisure craft or ships, their course shall not be impeded until they have been completely overtaken or a sufficient distance from them has been reached.
(f) Acts threatening the safety of other persons or other water leisure craft or ships, arbitrarily removing a water leisure craft's silencer, or startling others by producing loud roaring sounds are prohibited.

2. Craft speeds and other matters
(a) In areas within 20 meters of breakwaters, diving platforms, mooring facilities, or bridges, or in dangerous areas designated by the chief of a coast guard station or the head of a Si, Gun, or Gu, navigation shall be at a speed not exceeding 10 knots, and any separate navigation guidelines established by the chief of the coast guard station or the head of the Si, Gun, or Gu shall be followed.
(b) Power-driven water leisure craft fitted with special devices for artificially generating waves shall not be operated within 150 meters of any of the following places. This shall not apply where the craft is operated at a speed not exceeding 5 knots without artificially generating waves using the special devices fitted to it:
1) Mooring facilities;
2) Places where water leisure craft and equipment floating on the water, such as fixed inflatable tubes, are installed.

■ Enforcement Decree of the Water Leisure Safety Act [Annex 12] <Amended on December 16, 2025>
Designation Standards for Rafting Guide Education Institutions (Related to Article 26 (1) 2)

1. Standards for purpose of establishment: The applicant shall be an institution, organization, or corporation falling under either of the following:
(a) An institution or organization established for lifesaving on the water or lifesaving education;
(b) A corporation established to perform services related to water leisure safety.
2. Personnel standards: At least 5 instructors holding lifeguard or rafting guide qualifications under Article 26 (1) shall be employed.
3. Facility standards: All of the following facilities shall be provided:
(a) Classroom: An area of at least 65 square meters;
(b) Swimming pool: At least 20 meters long, with a minimum water depth of at least 1 meter and at least 5 lanes.
4. Equipment standards: Educational equipment determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard shall be provided.

Note: If rights to use facilities and equipment are secured through a lease agreement or similar arrangement and this is proved by documents, the relevant facilities and equipment shall be deemed provided for the term of that agreement.

■ Enforcement Decree of the Water Leisure Safety Act [Annex 13]
Standards for Imposing Penalty Surcharges (Related to Article 34)

1. General standards
(a) If there are two or more violations and the respective disposition standards under Annex 6, 8, or 10 differ, the heavier disposition standard shall apply. If two or more disposition standards under Annex 6, 8, or 10 all require suspension of services, the penalty surcharge replacing the heavier disposition standard shall be increased by up to one-half, without exceeding the sum of the penalty surcharges replacing the respective disposition standards.
(b) Aggravated standards for imposing penalty surcharges based on the number of violations shall apply where a disposition imposing a penalty surcharge (including a disposition suspending business under Annex 6, 8, or 10; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Annex) has been received for the same violation within the most recent 1 year. The period shall be calculated by reference to the date the disposition imposing a penalty surcharge was received for the violation and the date on which the same violation committed again after that disposition was detected.
(c) When imposing an aggravated penalty surcharge under item (b), the applicable violation level shall be the level immediately following that of the disposition preceding the violation concerned (the highest level if two or more dispositions imposing penalty surcharges were imposed within the period under item (b)).
(d) The imposing authority may reduce the penalty surcharge under the individual standards in paragraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases. This shall not apply to a violator in arrears with penalty surcharges:
1) Where the violation is recognized as resulting from minor negligence or error;
2) Where the violator immediately corrects or remedies the violation and thereby ends the state of noncompliance with the Act;
3) Where a reduction in the penalty surcharge is otherwise considered necessary in view of the nature, severity, motive, consequences, or other circumstances of the violation.
(e) The imposing authority may increase the penalty surcharge under the individual standards in paragraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases. Even when increased, the surcharge shall not exceed the maximum penalty surcharge under Article 25 (1) of the Act:
1) Where the nature and severity of the violation are serious and the harm to users or others is recognized as substantial;
2) Where the state of noncompliance with the Act has lasted at least 6 months;
3) Where an increase in the penalty surcharge is otherwise considered necessary in view of the severity, motive, consequences, or other circumstances of the violation.

